Title:
Writing Self‑Mutating Malware: Building a Polymorphic Engine (Veil64) and a Metamorphic ELF Infector (Morpheus)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post explains how self-mutating malware defeats signature-based detection by generating functionally equivalent but bytewise-different code across executions/infections. It contrasts oligomorphic (rotating decryptors), polymorphic (auto-generated decryptor + encrypted payload), and metamorphic (full-body rewriting without a fixed decryptor) approaches, with historical context and practical implementation notes. The author presents two concrete Linux x86_64 examples: “Veil64,” a polymorphic decryptor/stub generator that emits raw machine code with randomized registers, algorithm variants, garbage insertion, and control-flow disruption; and “Morpheus,” a metamorphic ELF infector that rewrites its own code by scanning for known “JUNK” patterns and replacing them with equivalent variants, plus XOR-encrypted strings and basic anti-debug ideas. Key techniques include runtime key derivation (RDTSC/RSP/RIP), XorShift PRNG, RIP-relative addressing for ASLR safety, and syscall-stub polymorphism (multiple ways to set up `mmap`). Useful primarily for red teamers, malware researchers, and reverse engineers studying evasion mechanics; secondarily for blue teamers building robust detections beyond static signatures. It’s interesting because it shows byte-level codegen and mutation constraints (semantic preservation, jump fixups, stability/bloat tradeoffs) with substantial assembly excerpts.

Technical Focus:
- Polymorphic decryptor generation (register renaming, algorithm variants, junk insertion)
- Runtime entropy/key derivation (RDTSC, stack pointer, RIP/ASLR) and PRNG (XorShift)
- On-the-fly x86_64 machine-code emission (ModRM encoding, opcode synthesis)
- Metamorphic rewriting via pattern-based mutation points (“JUNK” macros) in ELF binaries
- Linux x86_64 direct syscalls (`mmap`, `getdents64`, `execve`, `fork`, `wait4`) and RIP-relative addressing

Use Cases:
- Generate high-variance shellcode/loader stubs to stress-test signature-based detections
- Study polymorphic vs metamorphic design tradeoffs and failure modes (correctness, bloat, stability)
- Build lab PoCs for reverse-engineering practice on self-modifying/self-rewriting code
- Develop defensive detections focused on behavior, memory protections, and syscall patterns rather than byte signatures
- Evaluate sandbox/emulator weaknesses against timing/entropy-dependent unpacking

Keywords:
polymorphic engine, metamorphic malware, self-mutating code, x86_64 assembly, Linux ELF infector, signature evasion, decryptor stub, runtime code generation, ModRM encoding, RIP-relative addressing, ASLR, RDTSC, XorShift PRNG, garbage code injection, control-flow obfuscation, mmap syscall, getdents64, execve, anti-analysis, string XOR encryption